00:17:20.169 --> 00:17:24.616
some are using seminar style format,
they're using didactic.

00:17:24.616 --> 00:17:28.535
Are you a visual learner,
are you a more verbal learner,

00:17:28.535 --> 00:17:31.647
in Stanford there's
a very good job of that.

00:17:31.647 --> 00:17:37.622
Three, the sheer amount of resources here,
I think, is unrivaled, worldwide.

00:17:37.622 --> 00:17:45.494
And that might be on a per capita basis
because they invest in smaller class size.

00:17:45.494 --> 00:17:50.871
But I do think the choice of classes is
also intentional to be able to ensure

00:17:50.871 --> 00:17:56.360
that you're almost buried in resources,
and that's fantastic.

00:17:56.360 --> 00:18:00.928
And if you're coming from Africa like
me and you don't really like cold,

00:18:00.928 --> 00:18:06.110
there's something to be said about
the wonderful weather in California.

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:08.480
Yeah, so, I could go on and on.

00:18:08.480 --> 00:18:12.526
But this just gives you a sense of how
I think Stanford compares to many of

00:18:12.526 --> 00:18:14.820
the other programs that I've been in.

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:18.230
I think many people could
learn from Stanford.

00:18:19.750 --> 00:18:23.590
&gt;&gt; And then, Michelle,
you did your undergrad here in the US.

00:18:23.590 --> 00:18:28.813
Did your undergrad compared to
a graduate program knowing that you went

00:18:28.813 --> 00:18:34.220
to Haverford, a liberal arts college
in Philadelphia on the East Coast.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:39.042
Can you talk a little bit about how
those two experiences might compare and

00:18:39.042 --> 00:18:43.786
what this folks, who might also be
attending a small liberal arts school

00:18:43.786 --> 00:18:46.768
could expect coming to
a school like the GSB.

00:18:46.768 --> 00:18:48.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so, it's funny.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:52.772
I'm trying to think back and it makes
me feel like it was like, decades ago,

00:18:52.772 --> 00:18:53.935
[LAUGH] this is funny.

00:18:53.935 --> 00:18:56.763
But I think the biggest difference is,
well,

00:18:56.763 --> 00:18:59.382
maybe let me start with the similarities.

00:18:59.382 --> 00:19:02.658
I think the similarities is
Haverford was a pretty small school.

00:19:02.658 --> 00:19:08.939
So, overall, the entire student body
was something like 1,000 students.

00:19:08.939 --> 00:19:13.390
GSB each class is about 400, so,
times three you are getting about 800.

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:16.472
So the size of the communities
are pretty similar.

00:19:16.472 --> 00:19:20.288
But I think what I would say
the biggest differences are is one,

00:19:20.288 --> 00:19:25.410
when you go into undergrad, you're just
at a different point in your life, right?

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:30.363
You're probably, that's the first time
maybe you've left your parents home and so

00:19:30.363 --> 00:19:33.640
it's a very foreign environment for
a lot of people.

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:37.459
I think even the people who are maybe
based locally, it's like the first time

00:19:37.459 --> 00:19:41.064
you're really in a different
environment from your home environment.

00:19:41.064 --> 00:19:44.153
And so, I remember for me because that
was my first time coming to Kenya,

00:19:44.153 --> 00:19:45.533
it was very similar to Abiodun.

00:19:45.533 --> 00:19:50.409
I think that's when I had to deal with
the issues around like accents and

00:19:50.409 --> 00:19:56.014
being heard and getting used to the class
environment, how grading is done here,

00:19:56.014 --> 00:19:59.753
the relationships that you
have with your professor,

00:19:59.753 --> 00:20:03.110
that's why I think I had my culture shock.

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:08.008
And so as a result I think when I went
into Business School, it didn't feel

00:20:08.008 --> 00:20:12.610
as much of a tough transition
as I think undergrad did for me.

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:15.822
I think the other difference that I
would call out is because you're sort

00:20:15.822 --> 00:20:17.836
of in a different point in your life,
right?

00:20:17.836 --> 00:20:21.777
When you get into Business School,
you have some work experience.

00:20:21.777 --> 00:20:25.725
For a lot of people you've worked,
anywhere between two to four years, you've

00:20:25.725 --> 00:20:29.686
been out in the world, you've essentially
earned your own money in some cases.

00:20:29.686 --> 00:20:32.056
And so
your approach to both the academic and

00:20:32.056 --> 00:20:35.270
social environment in
Business School is very different.

00:20:35.270 --> 00:20:37.570
So Business School is a lot more social.

00:20:37.570 --> 00:20:39.100
It's a lot more network-focused.

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:43.484
It's a lot more focused around how you're
building longer term relationships with

00:20:43.484 --> 00:20:44.790
your classmates.

00:20:44.790 --> 00:20:49.903
Whereas I think undergrad is more academic
in nature, in terms of the focus.

00:20:49.903 --> 00:20:55.042
And so, I think at the end of the day,
what I enjoyed about both experiences is,

00:20:55.042 --> 00:21:00.670
because they're small, you do have that
closeness that Joshua mentioned, right?

00:21:00.670 --> 00:21:03.340
The community, the inclusiveness.

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:09.220
But because you're at different points in
your life, what you get out of a business

00:21:09.220 --> 00:21:14.680
school environment is, I think,
more longer term in terms of your network,

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:20.630
and your career than probably undergrad,
just based on my experience.

00:21:20.630 --> 00:21:24.348
&gt;&gt; Nice, and then so this next question
maybe we can start with you, as well,

00:21:24.348 --> 00:21:25.260
Michelle.

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:27.680
So the question comes from Kashira,

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:31.894
who has a question about the MBA
experience, how did it change or

00:21:31.894 --> 00:21:36.240
impact your perception of how
you view business in Africa?

00:21:36.240 --> 00:21:39.971
Earlier you mentioned some of
the different cultural elements and

00:21:39.971 --> 00:21:42.060
how you interact with others.

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:44.556
Can you tell us a little bit
more about that Michelle, and

00:21:44.556 --> 00:21:47.115
then we'll open it up to the panel.

00:21:47.115 --> 00:21:51.624
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I would say one of the reasons
I actually was really excited to go to

00:21:51.624 --> 00:21:56.205
Stanford once I got in is because, I
think Stanford is one of the few business

00:21:56.205 --> 00:21:59.568
schools in the US that is taking a very,
let's call it,

00:21:59.568 --> 00:22:05.060
mature partnership type of approach to
how they think about African businesses.

00:22:05.060 --> 00:22:07.600
And I think we see this both
in terms of the classes.

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:11.650
So there's a lot of, just case studies
that are focused on African countries.

00:22:11.650 --> 00:22:14.875
I remember my first strategy
class was on Equity Bank,

00:22:14.875 --> 00:22:17.407
which is one of the biggest
banks in Kenya.

00:22:17.407 --> 00:22:22.099
There's a lot of classes focused around
how you navigate some of the tougher sort

00:22:22.099 --> 00:22:26.584
of regulatory environments that we tend
to see in a lot of African countries,

00:22:26.584 --> 00:22:27.139
as well.

00:22:27.139 --> 00:22:31.295
And then, of course,
there is the Seed Network.

00:22:31.295 --> 00:22:35.680
That is a program at Stanford
that works directly to support

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:40.690
building leadership and
business skills with African companies.

00:22:40.690 --> 00:22:42.731
And they have a presence,
I believe, in Ghana.

00:22:42.731 --> 00:22:45.884
They have a presence in Kenya, and

00:22:45.884 --> 00:22:50.358
they work with a cohort
of companies every year.

00:22:50.358 --> 00:22:54.183
And so, being able to tap into
all of those resources during

00:22:54.183 --> 00:22:57.943
my Business School experience,
for me, was fantastic.

00:22:57.943 --> 00:23:02.023
Because even though I hadn't necessarily
spent a lot of time working on

00:23:02.023 --> 00:23:06.987
the continent, it gave me a sense for what
that could look like, because I'm learning

00:23:06.987 --> 00:23:11.203
more about the ins and outs of strategy
making within African countries,

00:23:11.203 --> 00:23:13.806
exploring these regulatory environments.

00:23:13.806 --> 00:23:16.585
And then with the Seed Network,
specifically,

00:23:16.585 --> 00:23:19.924
I had a chance to do an internship
with a Nigerian company.

00:23:19.924 --> 00:23:24.867
So, I was able to work with a Nigerian
makeup company the summer between first

00:23:24.867 --> 00:23:26.016
and second year.

00:23:26.016 --> 00:23:29.330
Which was just a fantastic
experience because, one,

00:23:29.330 --> 00:23:33.158
you are kind of then in the heart
of a business on the continent.

00:23:33.158 --> 00:23:36.162
You get to see the ins and outs,
you get to see the challenges, and

00:23:36.162 --> 00:23:39.420
you get to learn from the most
senior leaders in that company.

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:43.431
And so, before Business School,
the idea of, let's say,

00:23:43.431 --> 00:23:47.836
moving back home and engaging in
business was in my mind, I think,

00:23:47.836 --> 00:23:53.932
being able to then explore that fully
within the Business School experience In

00:23:53.932 --> 00:23:59.490
class as well as like on the ground within
internships made it a lot more real.

00:23:59.490 --> 00:24:04.538
So that as I think about long term
what could moving back home look like?

00:24:04.538 --> 00:24:08.045
I feel like I have a much better
understanding now versus before where it

00:24:08.045 --> 00:24:10.640
was, I think a lot more theoretical.

00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:14.831
&gt;&gt; Yeah, speaking of moving back home,
we have someone who did it on the panel.

00:24:14.831 --> 00:24:19.919
So, maybe Abiodun can you tell us
a little bit about like, did the GSB.

00:24:19.919 --> 00:24:24.668
How did that shape your view of
kind of working back on the continent?

00:24:24.668 --> 00:24:28.790
And how does it continue to
kind of impact your work today?

00:24:30.330 --> 00:24:31.836
&gt;&gt; So I tell you the so

00:24:31.836 --> 00:24:37.173
currently I'm special assistant
to the Ogun state governor.

00:24:37.173 --> 00:24:42.024
His excellency,
Governor Mallam Ahmad El-Rufai's and

00:24:42.024 --> 00:24:45.394
I basically focus on economic matters.

00:24:45.394 --> 00:24:50.591
I feel just believe it set me up for
success in that aspect.

00:24:50.591 --> 00:24:55.203
So while at GSB, I focused a lot
more on the social innovation.

00:24:55.203 --> 00:24:59.708
I took a lot of shine abroad classes and
I and the certificate for

00:24:59.708 --> 00:25:02.860
public management and social innovation.

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:08.120
So that set me up for
that line of my line of my career.

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:12.888
And after grad actually what is
between US with a Fintech company.

00:25:12.888 --> 00:25:18.158
And then even while working there,
I was very open to doing

00:25:18.158 --> 00:25:23.230
something that impacts like
the continent directly.

00:25:23.230 --> 00:25:29.234
I went there, I knew the opportunity was
going to come, was only a matter of time.

00:25:29.234 --> 00:25:31.988
So when it came down,
I just grabbed it and i moved back.

00:25:31.988 --> 00:25:38.454
And the governor actually, also assured
me of things are going to be fine.

00:25:38.454 --> 00:25:42.927
I'm going to be able to walk on programs
that can really see the impact.

00:25:42.927 --> 00:25:45.793
And a lot of that can actually
accomplished a little more.

00:25:45.793 --> 00:25:50.382
And what Stanford MBA is
still doing right now for me.

00:25:50.382 --> 00:25:56.190
My career is setting me apart from
every other person around to be honest.

00:25:56.190 --> 00:25:59.832
I have a better understanding of problems.

00:25:59.832 --> 00:26:03.160
I have better idea generation system.

00:26:03.160 --> 00:26:08.239
And I also shown out like
really quality word has been

00:26:08.239 --> 00:26:13.940
talked about in every part
of the governor's office.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:19.101
So pretty much try to put you in
a position where you can actually go far.

00:26:19.101 --> 00:26:21.312
Not only in business, but

00:26:21.312 --> 00:26:27.490
in other parts of the areas that
you may eventually choose to go to.

00:26:27.490 --> 00:26:33.534
For example, we are classes which
are beyond the market class for example.

00:26:33.534 --> 00:26:38.746
I give you an idea of what really
is the role of government for

00:26:38.746 --> 00:26:41.048
non market state actors.

00:26:41.048 --> 00:26:46.931
In ensuring the success of businesses
in a particular territory.

00:26:46.931 --> 00:26:51.405
So for me for now, if I related
with In a couple of businesses.

00:26:51.405 --> 00:26:56.326
So for example, we're just building
this very huge mall in town.

00:26:56.326 --> 00:27:02.505
And they will have to really get investors
like retail stores and all that.

00:27:02.505 --> 00:27:04.694
And I really have to work with
them to ensure that they succeed.

00:27:04.694 --> 00:27:06.680
They actually set up upon and succeed.

00:27:06.680 --> 00:27:09.052
Because that's where you get the benefit.

00:27:09.052 --> 00:27:13.269
And a lot of hour I was able to do that
was from the class life for example.

00:27:13.269 --> 00:27:15.700
Beyond the market class
that we did at Stanford.

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:20.311
So, really you give me
the awareness that as a government.

00:27:20.311 --> 00:27:24.172
Your role is not just step
on business environment.

00:27:24.172 --> 00:27:26.431
But to enable them to grow and

00:27:26.431 --> 00:27:31.150
then nourish them to become
billion dollar brands around.

00:27:31.150 --> 00:27:35.325
So it's been a really interesting
experience working back home.

00:27:35.325 --> 00:27:38.250
And I've actually opened even
a lot more doors to be honest.

00:27:38.250 --> 00:27:45.431
So for example, they actually got
admitted to the MPP program at Oxford.

00:27:45.431 --> 00:27:49.287
And the government is sponsoring
my attending the Masters in

00:27:49.287 --> 00:27:53.760
Public Policy Program at Sam University,
Oxford, for example.

00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:56.670
So these are the things that
you get benefits from based

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.270
on the fact that they
know that you're capable.

00:27:59.270 --> 00:28:03.970
That they just want to set you up for
success ongoing on that particular part.

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:08.457
So that's the it's been really
great experience for me so far.

00:28:08.457 --> 00:28:12.470
&gt;&gt; And Abiodun you mentioned
the certificate in public management.

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:14.461
We have a question here from Grace about.

00:28:14.461 --> 00:28:18.850
How does Stanford support folks, who are
looking to work in the nonprofit world?

00:28:18.850 --> 00:28:20.921
So I can share a couple of resources.

00:28:20.921 --> 00:28:23.472
And then we'll open it up to the panel for

00:28:23.472 --> 00:28:26.784
how they've seen people
working in the nonprofit.

00:28:26.784 --> 00:28:30.509
Social impact space kind of
taking shape on campus.

00:28:30.509 --> 00:28:32.784
We do offer a loan forgiveness program for

00:28:32.784 --> 00:28:35.610
folks who are working
in the nonprofit space.

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:38.976
So that is a wonderful opportunity
to really think about.

00:28:38.976 --> 00:28:42.520
How can I be a leader in any
field that I'm interested in?

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:44.235
Especially that nonprofit space.

00:28:44.235 --> 00:28:46.679
And not have to worry too much about.

00:28:46.679 --> 00:28:49.082
Like, what does that
debt burden look like?

00:28:49.082 --> 00:28:50.530
How am I going to pay it back?

00:28:50.530 --> 00:28:55.590
We need leaders in all fields regardless
of what potential incomes make.

00:28:55.590 --> 00:28:59.600
Another really great resource is
our center for social innovation.

00:28:59.600 --> 00:29:03.806
And so they are a one wonderful how
far different resources programs,

00:29:03.806 --> 00:29:04.950
alumni networks.

00:29:04.950 --> 00:29:10.130
That can really get you connected and even
provide additional sources of funding,

00:29:10.130 --> 00:29:13.988
internship opportunities and
connections and networking.

00:29:13.988 --> 00:29:17.207
To help accelerate your career and
have fun.

00:29:17.207 --> 00:29:22.084
And so we are dropping a couple of links
in the chat as we speak about those

00:29:22.084 --> 00:29:23.230
resources.

00:29:23.230 --> 00:29:27.500
But for anyone on the group, can you talk
a little bit more about like how you've

00:29:27.500 --> 00:29:29.365
seen folks interested in impact.

00:29:29.365 --> 00:29:32.439
Or kind of making that change in
the world come to life on campus.

00:29:37.219 --> 00:29:40.050
&gt;&gt; I can offer a couple of examples.

00:29:40.050 --> 00:29:46.694
So I think beyond what you talked about in
terms of the center of social innovation.

00:29:46.694 --> 00:29:50.015
I think, for graduating MBA to students,

00:29:50.015 --> 00:29:53.900
they also have a chance to apply for
a fellowship.

00:29:53.900 --> 00:29:56.974
I want to say it's the Africa fellowship,
maybe it's the wrong one.

00:29:56.974 --> 00:30:00.950
So you might need to look at
whether it's the right one.

00:30:00.950 --> 00:30:03.512
But there is a fellowship available for

00:30:03.512 --> 00:30:08.644
those that are interested in starting
their own social innovation ventures.

00:30:08.644 --> 00:30:13.024
And it helps to fund I think
a year of operations for

00:30:13.024 --> 00:30:17.310
the person to kind of
explore the model set it up.

00:30:17.310 --> 00:30:22.938
And so one of my classmates took advantage
of that set up a financial literacy and

00:30:22.938 --> 00:30:24.367
savings platform.

00:30:24.367 --> 00:30:28.290
Targeted at lower income
individuals here in the US.

00:30:28.290 --> 00:30:32.575
And so he was able to get a lot
of support from that program.

00:30:32.575 --> 00:30:37.751
For this from the center of social
innovation to get this venture up and

00:30:37.751 --> 00:30:38.548
running.

00:30:38.548 --> 00:30:40.513
For the last several years.

00:30:40.513 --> 00:30:43.960
So that's the example I threw out there.

00:30:43.960 --> 00:30:46.538
And, again, if I'm calling you
the wrong thing, please correct me.

00:30:46.538 --> 00:30:48.420
But I think that's what
it's called [LAUGH].

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:52.243
&gt;&gt; And then Josh,
I saw you hop on as well.

00:30:52.243 --> 00:30:55.760
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I guess I could jump in there.

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:57.791
I was actually going to mention
the Africa group fellowship.

00:30:57.791 --> 00:30:59.550
So yes, you got to name correctly.

00:31:00.650 --> 00:31:05.250
I think currently there's
also both a chime

00:31:05.250 --> 00:31:09.981
fellowship that supports social ventures.

00:31:09.981 --> 00:31:15.295
They actually just a lot, so
in terms of the curriculum

00:31:15.295 --> 00:31:22.471
that supports you to be able to think
through what you actually want to do.

00:31:22.471 --> 00:31:24.726
And refine both strategy.

00:31:24.726 --> 00:31:30.079
Your mission and
then in terms of practical experiences.

00:31:30.079 --> 00:31:35.180
So actually allow you to test
out some of your theories.

00:31:35.180 --> 00:31:43.467
In terms of connection to alumni who have
gone before you in many of these pathways.

00:31:43.467 --> 00:31:50.108
You are definitely not going to be
left alone with any of these ideas.

00:31:50.108 --> 00:31:56.850
I think Stanford's change lives, change
organizations, change the world, just.

00:31:56.850 --> 00:32:02.394
Comes to mind here, the elite talk, so
if you're interested in social innovation,

00:32:02.394 --> 00:32:05.170
I actually do think this
is the place to be.

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:13.110
&gt;&gt; And so we talk a lot about coming to
the GSB, looking for what's coming next.

00:32:13.110 --> 00:32:18.057
I think something that is also really
important that is very special

00:32:18.057 --> 00:32:22.223
to individuals is how do you
maintain the connections and

00:32:22.223 --> 00:32:26.940
relationships with the people you
had before coming to the GSB?

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:32.549
So here is a question from Fatogo about
how did you manage to stay in contact with

00:32:32.549 --> 00:32:35.394
your friends and family and
your home countries,

00:32:35.394 --> 00:32:38.280
even with a significant time difference?

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:40.367
Any tips, recommendations,

00:32:40.367 --> 00:32:44.790
reflections on how you were able
to maintain those connections?

00:32:48.160 --> 00:32:52.841
&gt;&gt; Maybe I'll start with mine
because mine is more recent than

00:32:52.841 --> 00:32:57.090
the other panelists and
then they could jump on.

00:32:57.090 --> 00:33:01.513
I think almost everything in
life when you want something,

00:33:01.513 --> 00:33:06.113
you just need to dedicate a little
bit of effort and time to it,

00:33:06.113 --> 00:33:10.730
I will not lie,
it is sometimes incredibly isolating.

00:33:10.730 --> 00:33:14.387
And especially in Africa where we
have morphe coming out culture,

00:33:14.387 --> 00:33:19.260
where I was used to just walking down
the road just knocking on my friends door.

00:33:19.260 --> 00:33:23.689
And kicking it in and saying,
hey, I want to sit on your couch,

00:33:23.689 --> 00:33:28.890
serve me, I don't want to cook you
feed me today, just stuff like that.

00:33:28.890 --> 00:33:33.048
So this is a very different atmosphere,

